<h1>Week 19</h1>

<p><strong>From:</strong> Lana</p>
<p><strong>Artist:</strong> The Smiths</p>
<p><strong>Album:</strong> <em>The Queen Is Dead</em></p>

<p>Kayla,</p>

<p>
This week I have <em>The Queen Is Dead</em> by The Smiths.
</p>

<p>
It’s ranked <strong>#113 on Rolling Stone’s Top 500 Albums</strong>, and it’s one of those records that has a really specific emotional gravity for me.
</p>

<p>
Morrissey could sing a phone book and it would sound beautiful.
</p>

<p>
This album reminds me of when I was living in Texas and attending group therapy in Austin. I had to drive a couple of hours there and back, and this was often playing in my car. It was a really dark time in my life, young adult angst and just trying to figure my shit out.
</p>

<p>
When I listen to it now, I connect with my younger self who was so lost but still hanging on.
</p>

<p>
My favorites are the darkest ones:
</p>

<ul>
<li>I Know It’s Over</li>
<li>Never Had No One Ever</li>
<li>There Is a Light That Never Goes Out</li>
</ul>

<p>
The lighter ones are:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Frankly, Mr. Shankly</li>
<li>Bigmouth Strikes Again</li>
</ul>

<p>
And a couple that feel especially interesting in today’s world:
</p>

<ul>
<li>The Boy with the Thorn in His Side</li>
<li>Vicar in a Tutu</li>
</ul>

<p>
I’m really interested to hear what you think.
</p>

<p>
Lana
</p>

